WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.200
This mussel is spurting babies!

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.190
Biologists at Cambridge University have just
discovered that females of this endangered

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:12.780
freshwater mussel species will move to shore,
attach themselves to the bank, then blast

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.369
a larva-filled stream of water a meter through
the air.

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:22.160
That’s 23 times their own body length, the
same as you spitting babies about 40 meters,

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.590
or 131 feet.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:29.560
The larva in the jet then parasitically attach
to the gills of passing fish and grow into

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:30.580
juveniles.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.640
No other species of mussel has been seen doing
this.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.710
So best of luck with your survival of your
species, ya baby squirtin’ little freak.

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:39.710
Vsauce!

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.410
One story down, five to go.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.079
This is Mind Blow!

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:47.140
Turn your phone into a makeshift fluorescent
microscope!

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.420
We owe much of what we know about cell and
tissue development in the human body to fluorescent

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:53.420
microscopes.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.730
It produces clear, beautiful images of tiny
cells that help highlight and identify specific

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.440
molecules and tiny biological structures.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.970
Well, a team of biologists at Winona State
University in Minnesota have just figured

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:11.320
out a way to use off-the-shelf components
to turn any smartphone into a fluorescence

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.640
microscope - for only about THIRTY BUCKS.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.590
Called a glowscope, its made up of an LED
flashlight, theatre filters, and a smartphone.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.830
This is an amazing tool for hands-on science
education.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:29.500
They even posted full instructions to build
your own online so you can finally study those

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.700
zebrafish embryos you’ve got in your garage.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.600
ATOMS CAN PLAY BASEBALL.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.680
With chilled rubidium.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.509
A team at the Korea Advanced Institute of
Science and Technology threw chilled rubidium

00:01:41.509 --> 00:01:48.219
atoms a distance of 4.2 micrometers -- which
would be a full kilometer for a real baseball.

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:53.540
They dropped the temperature to 0° Kelvin
-- just above absolute zero -- and tossed

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.290
it into two optical traps generated by 800
nanometer lasers.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.440
The atom pitcher wouldn’t sign with the
Yankees, let me tell ya that!

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.250
The rubidium “fastball” moved at 65 centimeters
per second, or 1.5 miles per hour.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:12.370
But it’s not actually about replicating
baseball -- testing and refining this technology

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:17.890
can eventually make quantum computers millions
of times faster than what we have today.

